.Dd June 7, 2026
.Dt XTC_FS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_fs_open ,
.Nm xtc_fs_close ,
.Nm xtc_fs_pread ,
.Nm xtc_fs_pwrite ,
.Nm xtc_fs_fsync ,
.Nm xtc_fs_fdatasync ,
.Nm xtc_fs_ftruncate ,
.Nm xtc_fs_fsize ,
.Nm xtc_fs_stat ,
.Nm xtc_fs_exists ,
.Nm xtc_fs_unlink ,
.Nm xtc_fs_rename ,
.Nm xtc_fs_mkdir ,
.Nm xtc_fs_rmdir ,
.Nm xtc_fs_tmpdir ,
.Nm xtc_fs_mkstemp ,
.Nm xtc_fs_dir_open ,
.Nm xtc_fs_dir_next ,
.Nm xtc_fs_dir_close
.Nd portable synchronous filesystem helpers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_fs.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_open "const char *path" "uint32_t flags" "int *out_fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_close "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_pread "int fd" "void *buf" "size_t n" "int64_t off" "size_t *out_done"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_pwrite "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t n" "int64_t off" "size_t *out_done"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_fsync "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_fdatasync "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_ftruncate "int fd" "int64_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_fsize "int fd" "int64_t *out_size"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_stat "const char *path" "xtc_fs_stat_t *out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_exists "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_unlink "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_rename "const char *from" "const char *to"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_mkdir "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_rmdir "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_tmpdir "char *buf" "size_t cap"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_mkstemp "char *tmpl" "int *out_fd"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_open "const char *path" "xtc_fs_dir_t **out"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_next "xtc_fs_dir_t *d" "const char **out_name"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_close "xtc_fs_dir_t *d"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions are a small, uniform surface over the platform's file
API (POSIX file descriptors and directories, or the Win32 equivalents),
so callers need not re-roll
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
directory walks, and the per-platform quirks
.Pf ( Dv F_FULLFSYNC
versus
.Xr fdatasync 2
versus
.Fn _commit ,
.Xr mkstemp 3
versus
.Fn _mktemp_s ,
.Vt dirent
versus
.Fn FindFirstFile ) .
.Pp
Files are plain
.Vt int
file descriptors, so a handle returned by
.Fn xtc_fs_open
or
.Fn xtc_fs_mkstemp
composes directly with the asynchronous
.Xr xtc_aio 3
operations and with raw
.Xr read 2 /
.Xr write 2 .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_fs_open
opens
.Fa path
according to
.Fa flags ,
a bitwise OR of
.Dv XTC_FS_READ ,
.Dv XTC_FS_WRITE ,
.Dv XTC_FS_CREATE ,
.Dv XTC_FS_TRUNC ,
.Dv XTC_FS_APPEND ,
and
.Dv XTC_FS_EXCL ,
returning the descriptor in
.Fa *out_fd .
.Fn xtc_fs_pread
and
.Fn xtc_fs_pwrite
perform positioned I/O, retrying on
.Er EINTR
and
.Er EAGAIN
and looping until
.Fa n
bytes are transferred or, for a read, end of file is reached; the byte
count is returned in
.Fa *out_done
(a short read is end of file, not an error).
.Fn xtc_fs_fsync
makes data and metadata durable;
.Fn xtc_fs_fdatasync
makes the data durable (the page- and log-flush hot path).
.Pp
.Fn xtc_fs_stat
fills
.Fa out
with the size, last-modified time (nanoseconds since the epoch), and a
directory flag.
.Fn xtc_fs_exists
returns 1 if
.Fa path
exists and 0 otherwise.
.Fn xtc_fs_rename
replaces
.Fa to
with
.Fa from
atomically.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_fs_tmpdir
writes the temporary directory into
.Fa buf ,
honoring
.Ev TMPDIR ,
.Ev TMP ,
and
.Ev TEMP
(and falling back to
.Pa /tmp ,
or
.Fn GetTempPath
on Windows), with any trailing separator trimmed.
.Fn xtc_fs_mkstemp
creates and opens a unique file from a
.Fa tmpl
ending in
.Dq XXXXXX .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_open ,
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_next ,
and
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_close
iterate a directory's entries, skipping
.Dq \&.
and
.Dq \&.. ;
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_next
sets
.Fa *out_name
to
.Dv NULL
at the end of the directory.
.Pp
These are blocking calls.
On a fiber loop, prefer
.Xr xtc_aio 3
for the read/write hot path and use these for open, stat, rename, and
directory management.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each function returns
.Dv XTC_OK
(zero) on success or a negative
.Vt xtc_err_t
code on failure:
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a bad argument,
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
for an absent path,
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure, or
.Dv XTC_E_IO
for an underlying I/O error.
.Fn xtc_fs_exists
returns 1 or 0 and
.Fn xtc_fs_dir_close
returns nothing.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_aio 3 ,
.Xr xtc_strerror 3
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The XTC Project
.Sh CAVEATS
On Windows the file descriptors are C run-time descriptors;
.Fn xtc_fs_pread
and
.Fn xtc_fs_pwrite
use overlapped I/O internally for positioned access and do not disturb
a file pointer.
.Fn xtc_fs_fdatasync
and
.Fn xtc_fs_fsync
both map to
.Fn _commit ,
which flushes data and metadata.
